AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude de cohorte (*Pain Management*, nov. 2026, 31 patients / 35 genoux, 91 % de gonarthrose sévère Kellgren-Lawrence 3–4) démontrant une baisse significative des douleurs (marche, repos, nuit) et une diminution de la synovite en IRM (Hoffa-synovitis / effusion-synovitis) sans aggravation des lésions osseuses sous-chondrales (BML) ni de l'arthrose à 3 mois. | Points clés : Efficacité antalgique rapide dans les gonarthroses sévères KL 3–4 non opérables ou en attente de PTG (-24 points d'EVA à la marche, p < 0,05). ; Sécurité articulaire démontrée en IRM : aucune aggravation des œdèmes osseux sous-chondraux (BML), de l'interligne (KL) ni de l'alignement mécanique. ; Réduction concomitante des scores IRM de synovite de Hoffa et d'épanchement articulaire grâce à la reprise de la mobilité. ; Aucune complication grave observée sous guidage échographique strict. | Pratique Cochin : Directement lié aux séances de « test anesthésique sous écho + radiofréquence des nerfs géniculés pour gonarthrose » programmées à l'Hôpital Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] OBJECTIVE: To evaluate short-term clinical and imaging outcomes after ultrasound-guided cooled radiofrequency ablation (CRFA) in patients predominantly with advanced knee osteoarthritis (KOA). METHODS: This retrospective cohort analysis included prospectively recorded data from 31 patients (35 knees; age, 73.6 ± 8.8 years; Kellgren-Lawrence [KL] grade 3 or 4 in 91%). Outcomes at baseline and 3 months included visual analog scale (VAS) pain, Knee Injury and Osteoarthritis Outcome Score (KOOS), KL grade, mechanical axis percentage, and MRI Osteoarthritis Knee Score assessments of bone marrow lesions (BMLs), Hoffa-synovitis, and effusion-synovitis. Sensitivity analysis retained the first-treated knee in bilateral cases. RESULTS: VAS changes were -9.3 for rest pain, -24.0 for walking pain, and -15.0 for night pain (all p < 0.05). Walking VAS decreased by at least 30% in 65.7% and by at least 50% in 42.9% of knees. Four KOOS subscales improved, including KOOS Pain by 12.8 points (p < 0.001). No significant group-level changes were observed in KL grade, alignment, or BML scores. Hoffa-synovitis and effusion-synovitis scores decreased. No serious procedure-related adverse events were documented. CONCLUSION: CRFA was associated with improved pain and function at 3 months without group-level worsening in KL grade, alignment, or BML scores. Longer-term controlled studies are required.
