TI  - Management of sacral fragility fractures leading to spinopelvic dissociation: a scoping review
AU  - Pope T
AU  - Wright E
AU  - Taylor P
AU  - Evans D
AU  - Daurka J
JO  - European journal of orthopaedic surgery & traumatology : orthopedie traumatologie
PY  - 2026
VL  - 36
IS  - 1
SP  - 412
DO  - 10.1007/s00590-026-04984-8
AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Revue systématique PRISMA-ScR (*Eur J Orthop Surg Traumatol*, oct. 2026, 8 études / 235 patients) consacrée aux fractures bilatérales postérieures du sacrum par insuffisance osseuse (fractures en H/U et Rommens IVb), confirmant que le vissage percutané est la technique de référence après échec du traitement médical pour restaurer la mobilité et soulager la douleur. | Points clés : Les fractures sacrées bilatérales par insuffisance (Rommens IVb / en U) entraînent une instabilité spino-pelvienne souvent rebelle au repos médical. ; Le vissage percutané ilio-sacré / trans-sacré est la technique mini-invasive de référence pour rétablir la continuité mécanique spino-pelvienne. ; Une intervention précoce en cas d'impossibilité de mise en charge accélère la récupération fonctionnelle et l'autonomie. ; En os très déminéralisé ou irradié, l'augmentation cimentée (vissage cimenté au scanner ou sous scopie Allura) prévient le desserrement mécanique. | Pratique Cochin : En lien direct avec les « vissages percutanés du bassin et sacroplasties (Scanner / Scopie Allura) » pour fractures sacrées par insuffisance ou post-radiques à Cochin. || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Purpose Fragility fractures affecting the sacrum present an increasing challenge to orthopaedic practice, due to limited comparative data and the absence of consensus on optimal management. U-Type and Rommens Type IVb fractures may result in a bilateral pattern of spinopelvic dissociation with possible neurological injury. This scoping review aims to summarise the current principles of care, indications for surgery, and outcomes of operative versus non-operative treatment for low-energy bilateral posterior sacral fractures (BPSFs). Methods This scoping review adhered to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses extension for Scoping Reviews (PRISMA-ScR). A systematic search of electronic databases identified studies including ≥ 10 patients with Bilateral Posterior Sacral Fractures (BPSFs) comparing operative and non-operative approaches. Two reviewers independently screened titles, abstracts, and full texts against predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria, resolving disagreements by consensus. Data were extracted using a standardised form and summarised narratively with reference to mobility, quality of life, and patient-reported outcome measures. From 421 studies screened, eight met inclusion criteria. Results From 421 screened records, eight studies including 235 patients met inclusion criteria. Operative management resulted from failed non-operative care in most cases. Percutaneous screw fixation was the most common surgical technique utilised. Although one study reported improved outcomes with early fixation, considerable heterogeneity in surgical approach and timing was observed with a similar lack of consensus on non-operative regimes and their point of 'failure'. Conclusion Operative management may enhance pain relief, mobility, and function in selected patients with BPSFs, though with notable implant-related risks. Careful patient selection remains key, and further prospective studies are needed to establish optimal non-operative management and those who would benefit from early operative intervention.
