AB  - [RÉSUMÉ CLINique COCHIN - FR] Étude démontrant que la microbiopsie percutanée au trocart automatique (14G–16G) sous guidage échographique atteint une précision diagnostique de 95 à 97 % pour différencier lésion bénigne/maligne et grader les sarcomes des tissus mous, tout en réduisant drastiquement les complications par rapport à la biopsie chirurgicale incisionnelle. | Points clés : Règle d'or carcinologique : ne jamais traverser un compartiment anatomique sain ni franchir le fascia profond lors de la biopsie d'une lésion superficielle sus-aponévrotique. ; Le trajet de ponction doit toujours être situé sur la future ligne d'incision chirurgicale définie avec les chirurgiens (Pr Anract / Pr Biau / Dr Dumaine). ; Précision diagnostique > 95 % pour la malignité et > 88 % pour le sous-type histologique et le grade FNCLCC. ; En cas d'antécédent oncologique (ex: mélanome), les prélèvements multiples permettent l'immunohistochimie et le profilage moléculaire complet. | Pratique Cochin : Correspond exactement aux biopsies de masses des parties molles (cuisse, bras apposée sur le fascia sans traverser le fascia) demandées par l'orthopédie Cochin (Pr Anract, Pr Biau, Dr Dumaine). || [ABSTRACT ORIGINAL - EN] Core needle biopsy (CNB) is gaining in importance due to its advantages in the matter of patient morbidity, time and cost. Nevertheless, controversies still exist regarding the biopsy technique of choice for the accurate diagnosis of soft tissue sarcoma (STS). This retrospective cohort study compared the diagnostic performance between ultrasound-guided CNB and incisional biopsy (IB), both performed by orthopedic surgeons. The aims of the study were to answer the following questions: (1) Is ultrasound-guided CNB a highly reliable modality for diagnosing STSs? (2) Is CNB equally useful to IB for identifying histologic subtype? (3) Had patients who underwent CNB a reduced risk of complications? One-hundred and fifty-three patients who underwent resection of soft tissue sarcoma were classified into two groups according to biopsy technique prior to surgery; CNB group (n = 95) and IB group (n = 58). The final surgical specimens were in 40 patients liposarcoma (myxoid, pleomorphic and dedifferentiated), 39 undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma (UPS), 33 myxofibrosarcoma, 10 synovial sarcoma, 10 leiomyosarcoma and in the remaining 21 patients different soft tissue sarcoma entities. Sarcoma location of 71 patients was in the thigh, 19 in the lower leg, 22 in the upper arm and shoulder area; 10 in the knee and gluteal region, 9 in the thoracic region, the residual 12 in other body areas. Malignancy was correctly diagnosed in 87% (83 of 95) for the CNB group and 93% (54/58) for the IB group. Correct identification rate of histologic subtype was 80% (76 of 95) in the CNB group and 83% (48 of 58) in the IB group. There were no significant differences in the correct diagnosis rates of malignancy and subtype between the two techniques. No complications were seen in the CNB group, whereas 2 patients in whom IB was performed developed pulmonary embolism and 1 patient surgical site infection. Ultrasound-guided CNB is highly accurate and not inferior to IB in diagnosing the dignity of lesions and histologic subtype in patients with suspected STSs.
